A||||6||maryjane||Paint & Body Gurus needed||||1||
Z||000000||maryjane||09-09-2001||07:47 PM||||Got a "little scrape" on the left frt fender,<BR>all the way down to the 'plastic'. Not much exp with this sort of thing, but thought I'd try it. Otherwise, Jane is going to take it & have it fixed at a shop. Wouldn't mind it, but the $ will have to come out of my rec funds.(meaning food, clothing, fish bait, you know. unimportant stuff)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/booboo.jpg">  <BR>Fender isn't cracked, just peeled the paint off. Flexible bumper repair stuff to fill &<BR>rattle can paint with cl coat? any suggestions? Thanks<BR><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||09-09-2001||08:16 PM||||<BR>1) Take a 120 grit to the scratch and about 1" surrounding area. Scuff it up, don't deepen the hole.<P>2) Lay on a coat of decent vinyl-based filler to the bad area. (Although if anyone could recommend a flexible filler, I'd like to know it. This is how I repair METAL cars. Should still work, though)<P>3) When the putty is half-set (not tacky, but still fairly soft) take a putty file to it and rough-shape it.<P>4) Let it harden, and take a 320 grit WET paper ON A BLOCK to the patched area. Sand it flush with the rest of the body. use your fingers, not your eyes to gauge if it's smooth. This WILL take a while. Be patient. <P>5) Take a 400 grit, and feather the repair area, then scuff the paint down on the whole panel.<P>6) Mask the rest of the car carefully, but leave the panel bare. Use a decent urethane  primer, and lay a nice light, even coat on the whole panel. Let it dry fully. Using a 400 grit paper on a (preferably) foam block, lightly scuff down that layer. Repeat about 3-4 times until the panel feels nice and uniform. <P>7) Drive the damn thing to Maaco (or whatever) and have them paint that fender. total cost, ~$50 bucks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>You've done all the expensive, time consuming stuff. The only reason to get a pro to do the topcoat is that they'll have a dust-free booth and a spray gun. It'll look SO much better than if you'd done it with spray-bombs...||||reg||7
Z||000002||fiero_hunter85||09-09-2001||09:50 PM||||i always use flexable urethane bumper repair.<BR>it worked good on my 88 coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-09-2001||11:20 PM||||Hard to say? is the pannel broken or just skinned.<P>If it's just skinned, clean up the area. scuff the tan area in the photo with 60-80 grit paper. Then skin it over with glass reinforced body filler.<P>the coarse papar will give the filler a better bite. A thin layer of filler is quite flexible. Filler cracking is usually caused when people use filler too thick or don't clean the surface well enough.<P>If you catch the filler in about mid set, you can do allot of shaping work very easily with wood working scrapers. In this case you need only a single straight edge scrapper. you can get them anyplace. They are normally used for scraping paint and light shaping of wood projects. A quick scrap durring filler setup saves large amounts of sanding.<P>Another important item.... mix the filler carefully. Avoid wipping air into it. If you've never used filler before practice on scrap material before trying to apply it to the car. This stuff is nowhere near a simple to work with as most people claim if you want quality work. Practice mixing as well as spreading to avoid air bubbles. This will also give you some patches to try scraping and sanding on before you mess with the car.<P>If the panel actually cracked, you're looking at a full fiberglass repair job. Also not easy for quality work.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||8Ball||09-09-2001||11:27 PM||||Me personally would check local junkyards for a white fender  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The easy way out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||09-09-2001||11:59 PM||||no cracks, some tipsy soul scraped it with a  bumper of some kind and deflected the fender enough to peel the paint down to the skin. Good thing the fender flexed. I'll try some practice pieces first. I've got an old qtr pnl to practice on. Thanks all. No white fenders around, besides, I've gotta put a little of all ya'lls info to use. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||09-10-2001||12:23 PM||||If i was fixing it in my shop, for that light a damage, id sand the whole area down with 80. use regular body filler (its flexible enough for a rigid fender) Primer it and block sand, reprime as necessary then paint fender. BTW most MAACO shops wont paint panels, only whole car, they dont want to have to worry about mismatching. Spray can on one fenders ok, you can rub it out to look as good as rest of car.||||reg||1
